## .NET Performance Analysis: CaseTransformExtensions.cs

### Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.ToLower()/.ToUpper()` | 2 |
| `.Substring(` | 1 (2 calls on line) |
| `RegexOptions.Compiled` | 7 |
| `GeneratedRegex` | 3 |
| `new Regex(` in constructor | 1 |
| `sealed class` | 0 |
| Unsealed non-static classes | 1 of 1 |

---

### Findings

#### 1. Triple Regex Replace Chain + `.ToLower()` in `Underscore()` (1 instance)
🔴 **Critical** — hot path, called millions of times

**Impact:** 4 intermediate string allocations per call (3 regex replaces + 1 `.ToLower()`). At millions of calls, this is massive GC pressure.
**Files:** CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L117-121
**Fix:** Replace with a single-pass `StringBuilder` or `string.Create()` approach that walks the string once, inserting underscores at boundaries and lowercasing in-place. Also use `.ToLowerInvariant()` if regex approach is kept (correctness + slight perf).

```csharp
// Optimized single-pass approach
public static string Underscore(this string input)
{
    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length + 4);
    for (int i = 0; i < input.Length; i++)
    {
        char c = input[i];
        if (c == '-' || c == ' ')
        {
            sb.Append('_');
        }
        else if (char.IsUpper(c) && i > 0 &&
                 (char.IsLower(input[i - 1]) ||
                  (i + 1 < input.Length && char.IsUpper(input[i - 1]) && char.IsLower(input[i + 1]))))
        {
            sb.Append('_');
            sb.Append(char.ToLowerInvariant(c));
        }
        else
        {
            sb.Append(char.ToLowerInvariant(c));
        }
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

#### 2. Per-Match Allocations in `Pascalize()` (1 instance)
